# Append-only index files: keep both sides of a concurrent append instead of
# raising a conflict. Two parallel rounds each add one line at the end, and
# git's built-in `union` driver concatenates both sides of the conflicting
# hunk rather than asking a human to pick.
#
# Deliberately scoped to that one index file and nothing else. A union merge
# on a prose file would silently duplicate a paragraph that both sides edited,
# which is worse than a conflict: a conflict is visible, a duplicate is not.
docs/ledger/INDEX.md merge=union

# Byte-compared fixtures: never let git rewrite their line endings.
#
# Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` in its *system* config, so a
# checkout on a Windows runner rewrites every LF in a "text" file to CRLF in
# the working tree. `update_script_golden_test.dart` compares the generated
# updater against this golden **as bytes** -- deliberately, because the BOM is
# half of what it pins (update_installer_test.dart's BOM test) and a string comparison would
# normalise that away -- while the generator always emits `\n`. So on Windows
# the golden on disk had CRLF, the generated script had LF, and the byte
# comparison failed at the first newline (offset 77: <10> against <13>).
#
# `-text`, not `binary`: `binary` is `-text -diff`, and the golden is a
# reviewable PowerShell script whose diff is worth reading.
#
# Scoped to the class rather than the one path, unlike the `merge=union` line
# above: a `.golden` file exists to be compared byte-for-byte, so eol
# conversion is wrong for every one of them, not just this one.
*.golden -text
